$4^2 \left[ \sin^2 \left( \frac{\pi}{16} \right) + \sin^2 \left( \frac{3\pi}{16} \right) \right] \left[ \cos^2 \left( \frac{\pi}{16} \right) \right]$

## Step 15:  Apply the half-angle trigonometric identity to the expression $(\sin^2 \theta + \sin^2 \alpha) = \frac{1 - \cos(2\theta)}{2} + \frac{1 - \cos(2\alpha)}{2} = \frac{2 - \cos(2\theta) - \cos(2\alpha)}{2}$  to $\sin^2 \left( \frac{\pi}{16} \right) + \sin^2 \left( \frac{3\pi}{16} \right)$
Using the identity, we get
